From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 350/488] soh: name the 16 address-named symbols defined in
 z_eff_ss_kakera.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 16 are defined in soh/src/overlays/effects/ovl_Effect_Ss_Kakera/z_eff_ss_kakera.c.

  * D_809AA530 -> sDragThresholds
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-mode distance thresholds the drag
    falloff functions compare against before scaling the acceleration down.

  * func_809A9818 -> EffectSsKakera_RandCentered
    deduced, confidence HIGH: returns a value uniformly distributed within
    the given range either side of the centre; its own osSyncPrintf calls it
    "randomD_sectionUniformity".

  * func_809A9BA8 -> EffectSsKakera_CheckObjectLoaded
    deduced, confidence HIGH: looks up the fragment's object bank and kills
    the particle if the object is missing or not yet loaded.

  * func_809A9C10 -> EffectSsKakera_UpdateRotation
    deduced, confidence HIGH: turns the fragment's velocity into per-axis
    spin rates, jittered by the randomness parameter.

  * func_809A9DC0 -> EffectSsKakera_ClearAccel
    deduced, confidence HIGH: zeroes the acceleration before the per-frame
    force passes accumulate into it.

  * func_809A9DD8 -> EffectSsKakera_DragNone
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the no-drag falloff: always returns 1.0.

  * func_809A9DEC -> EffectSsKakera_DragLinear
    deduced, confidence HIGH: falls off as the threshold over the distance
    once the distance passes the threshold.

  * func_809A9E28 -> EffectSsKakera_DragSquared
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the same falloff against the squared distance.

  * func_809A9E68 -> EffectSsKakera_DragSquared2
    deduced, confidence HIGH: a second entry point that forwards straight to
    EffectSsKakera_DragSquared; the dispatch table lists both.

  * func_809A9E88 -> EffectSsKakera_ApplyAttraction
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the first force pass: pulls the fragment back
    toward its origin at one of two strengths, chosen by the low bits of the
    behaviour flags.

  * func_809A9F10 -> EffectSsKakera_ApplyBuoyancy
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the second force pass: adds one of seven fixed
    vertical accelerations, chosen by the next bits of the behaviour flags.

  * func_809A9F4C -> EffectSsKakera_ApplyRepulsion
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the third force pass: pushes the fragment away
    from its origin at one of three strengths.

  * func_809A9FD8 -> EffectSsKakera_ApplyDrag
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the fourth pass: picks a falloff function from
    the behaviour flags and scales the accumulated acceleration by it, with
    a random jitter.

  * func_809AA0B8 -> EffectSsKakera_ApplyGravity
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the last pass: adds the particle's own gravity
    to the vertical acceleration.

  * func_809AA0EC -> EffectSsKakera_UpdateAccel
    deduced, confidence HIGH: clears the acceleration, measures the offset
    from the origin and runs the force passes in order, giving up once the
    fragment is more than 1000 units away.

  * func_809AA230 -> EffectSsKakera_UpdateGrounded
    deduced, confidence HIGH: stops the fragment dead once it has fallen
    below the floor, clearing its velocity, acceleration, gravity and spin.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
